```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .toast[data-state="closing"] {
    transition: transform 180ms cubic-bezier(0.3, 0, 1, 1), opacity 180ms cubic-bezier(0.3, 0, 1, 1);
    transform: translateY(16px);
    opacity: 0;
  }
}
```

Dismissing a toast is a permanent exit, so it accelerates away on an accelerate curve. The trade-off: the curve starts slow, so the first frames after the dismiss can read as lag; it stays short to keep that small.

```css
:root { --ease-in: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1); }
.toast[data-state="closing"] {
  transition: transform 160ms var(--ease-in), opacity 160ms var(--ease-in);
  transform: translateY(24px);
  opacity: 0;
}
```

You asked for it to fall away, so the exit uses the accelerate token. It starts slow, which can read as lag right after the click.

```css
.toast[data-state="closing"] {
  transition: translate 200ms var(--ease-in, cubic-bezier(0.55, 0, 1, 0.45)), opacity 200ms var(--ease-in, cubic-bezier(0.55, 0, 1, 0.45));
  translate: 0 20px;
  opacity: 0;
}
```

The exit accelerates as it leaves. Note the slow start: the first frames barely move, which can feel like lag.

